  "title": "rb 0032 manipulability",
  "slug": "rb-0032-manipulability",
  "public": true,
  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:rb-0032-manipulability",
  "domain": "robotics",
  "definition": "Manipulability is a scalar or ellipsoid measure of a robotic manipulator's capacity to move and exert forces in arbitrary directions from a given joint configuration. Introduced by Tsuneo Yoshikawa, the manipulability measure w = sqrt(det(J\u00b7J\u1d40)) quantifies how far a configuration is from kinematic singularity: higher values indicate greater dexterity, while w = 0 indicates a singular configuration where motion in at least one direction is lost. Manipulability is used in motion planning, redundancy resolution, and task-space control to avoid singular regions.",
